 <div class="card"><h2>What the words mean, physically</h2>
 <p>Every number below is a statement about water moving, not about computation. Before the results, what each term stands for in the sea:</p>
 <div class="scroll" style="max-height:none"><table>
 <tr><th>Term</th><th>What it is, physically</th></tr>
 <tr><td><b>Particle</b></td><td>A hypothetical floating object with no size, no sail area and no shape &mdash; a marker for a parcel of surface water plus a small wind push. Real debris has a hull, a waterline and a drag signature; this does not. It is the simplest possible floating thing.</td></tr>
-<tr><td><b>Release</b></td><td>The place and moment we put one in the water: eight latitudes from 33&deg;S to 45&deg;S, five longitudes from 85&deg;E to 98&deg;E, twelve objects scattered within about 5&nbsp;km of each cell centre, all at midnight UTC on 8 March 2014. <b>These positions are arbitrary sampling of the ocean, not candidate crash sites.</b></td></tr>
+<tr><td><b>Release</b></td><td>The specified initial position and time: eight latitudes from 33&deg;S to 45&deg;S, five longitudes from 85&deg;E to 98&deg;E, twelve objects scattered within about 5&nbsp;km of each cell centre, all at midnight UTC on 8 March 2014. <b>These positions are arbitrary sampling of the ocean, not candidate crash sites.</b></td></tr>
 <tr><td><b>1.2% wind slip</b></td><td>A partly submerged object sits in two fluids. The water carries it; the air above pushes the part that sticks out. The accepted shorthand from professional MH370 drift work is that the net effect is roughly 1.2% of the wind speed, added to the current. In a 10&nbsp;m/s wind that is 12&nbsp;cm/s &mdash; comparable to the currents themselves, which is why it matters.</td></tr>
 <tr><td><b>Tortuosity</b></td><td>Distance actually travelled through the water, divided by straight-line distance from start to finish. <b>It measures how much the ocean stirred an object versus how far it carried it.</b> A value of 1 is a straight drift. A value of 5 means the object sailed five kilometres of ocean for every kilometre of progress &mdash; it spent the year going round in eddies.</td></tr>
 <tr><td><b>Gate crossing</b></td><td>The moment an object drifted past a chosen line of longitude. 80&deg;E, 70&deg;E, 60&deg;E, 50&deg;E head toward Africa, R&eacute;union and Madagascar; 100&deg;E, 105&deg;E, 110&deg;E head toward Australia. Crossing a meridian is a fact about where the water went. <b>It is not an arrival, a landfall or a recovery.</b></td></tr>
-<tr><td><b>Domain exit</b></td><td>The object reached the edge of the ocean we have data for and we stopped watching. In survival-analysis language it is <i>censored</i> &mdash; the object did not finish, it left the experiment. It is emphatically not a beaching, and an object that exits westward has not reached Africa.</td></tr>
-<tr><td><b>Mask exit</b></td><td>The object entered a cell the ocean model marks as land or as having no valid velocity. Without a coastline product we cannot tell a genuine coast contact from a data hole, so these are reported separately and never called a beaching.</td></tr>
