 <p style="margin:0 0 10px">The main experiment samples a box between 33 and 45&deg;S. A fair question about it is whether the result is an artefact of that box &mdash; whether releasing material further along the 7th arc, where the arc runs away to the west, would send it west sooner. So the release design was replaced with twelve points spaced along the arc itself, from 46&deg;E to 105&deg;E, twelve objects at each, and nothing else was changed: same currents, same wind coupling, same 1,638-day window, same integrator.</p>
 <p style="margin:0 0 10px">The arc is not a line of longitude. Because it is a circle on the sphere centred near the equator at 64.5&deg;E, moving west along it also moves <i>south</i> &mdash; from 20&deg;S at 105&deg;E down to about 44&deg;S near 64&deg;E, then back north to 40.7&deg;S at 46&deg;E. Longitude along the arc is therefore a proxy for latitude, and latitude is what the ocean responds to.</p>
